<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>What is Proof by Contradiction and Why Do It?</title>
<page>
    

    <p>
        In a <term>proof by contradiction</term>, we
    </p>

    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Assume the negation
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Reduce to the absurd
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Conclude the negation could not have been true.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
        Some of the most consequential theorems in history were proved by
        contradiction.See
        <url href="https://philosophersguild.com/products/proof-is-in-the-pudding-bowls">https://philosophersguild.com/products/proof-is-in-the-pudding-bowls</url>:
        2 of the 4 proofs chosen for the bowls are proofs by contradiction.
    </p>

    <activity workspace="1in">
      <p>
            We typically prove the following two theorems by contradiction.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        There are infinitely many prime numbers.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The number <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is irrational.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        
      <p>      Why would you use proof by contradiction for these theorems?
      To answer this, you might discuss: (a) What is the negation and how might that be helpful for starting a proof? (b) What other proof techniques could you try and how would those start? </p>
    </activity>

    </page>

    <page>
        

    <paragraphs>
        <title>First Example</title>

        <p>
            You have already seen a proof by contradiction!  See <xref ref="thm-disjoint-congruence"/>
            which is a theorem about disjoint subsets. 
        </p>

        <p>
            The theorem and proof are repeated below. Read them, one sentence at a time, 
            and underline
            sentences that you think will be in every proof by contradiction.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <theorem>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A = \{x\in\mathbb{Z} : x\equiv 2 \pmod{4}\}</m> and
                <m>B = \{y\in\Z : y\equiv 3 \pmod{6}\}</m>.
                Then <m>A\cap B = \emptyset</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            We will use a proof by contradiction.
            That is, we assume
            <m>A =  \{x\in\mathbb{Z} : x\equiv 2 \pmod{4}\}</m> and
            <m>B = \{y\in\Z : y\equiv 3 \pmod{6}\}</m> and
            <m>A\cap B \neq \emptyset</m>.
            Since <m>A\cap B\neq \emptyset</m>, there exists <m>x\in\Z</m> such
            that <m>x\in A\cap B</m>.
            Since <m>x\in A</m>, <m>x\equiv 2\pmod{4}</m> and so
            <m>4\mid (x-2)</m>.
            This means there exists <m>k\in\mathbb{Z}</m> such that
            <m>x-2 = 4k</m>, or
            <md>
                x= 4k+2.
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Also, since <m>x\in B</m>, we know <m>x\equiv 3\pmod{6}</m>.
            So <m>6 \mid (x-3)</m> and thus there exists <m>j\in \mathbb{Z}</m>
            such that <m>x-3=6j</m> or
            <md>
                x=6j+3.
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Since <m>x=4k+2</m> and <m>x=6j+3</m>, by substitution and algebra
            we get,
            <md>
                <mrow>
            4k+2 \amp = 6j+3
          </mrow>
                <mrow> 4k \amp = 6j+1 </mrow>
                <mrow>2 (2k)\amp =2(3j)+1  </mrow>
            </md>
            By closure of integers under multiplication, <m>2k\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
            and <m>3j\in\mathbb{Z}</m>.
            Therefore <m>2(2k)</m> is an even integer and <m>2(3j)+1</m> is an
            odd integer.
            But this is a contradiction - there is no integer that is both even
            and odd!
            This contradicts our assumption that <m>x\in A\cap B</m>, and thus
            we conclude <m>A\cap B = \emptyset</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <remark>
        <p>
            In a proof by contradiction, you want to work towards something
            obviously absurd.
            <q>Even = Odd</q> is one example of a contradiction you might get.
            You also could get other contradictions in the proof above - can you
            think of a different one?
        </p>

        <p>
            We could have also phrased the above theorem as
            <q>For each integer <m>n</m>, if <m>n\equiv 2 \pmod{4}</m> then <m>n\not\equiv 3\pmod{6}</m>.</q>
            Why would it make sense to use contradiction for that theorem
            statement (and not, say, contrapositive)?
            What would change about the proof above?
        </p>
    </remark>

        </page>
</handout>